Static vs instance methods:
- static: belongs to the class, callable without an object: Math.sqrt()
- instance: belongs to an object, needs an object to call: student.getName()

Method overloading — same name, different parameter types:
    public int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
    public double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }

Java always passes by value:
- Primitive types: a copy is passed — the original is not changed.
- Object references: the reference is copied — the object's fields can be changed, but reassigning the parameter does not affect the original reference.

Varargs (variable number of arguments):
    public int sum(int... numbers) {
        int total = 0;
        for (int n : numbers) total += n;
        return total;
    }

Defining a class:
```java
    public class Student {
        // fields
        private String name;
        private int age;

        // constructor
        public Student(String name, int age) {
            this.name = name;
            this.age  = age;
        }

        // method
        public String getName() {
            return name;
        }

