```

Creating objects:
```java
    Student s1 = new Student("Alice", 20);
    Student s2 = new Student("Bob", 22);
    System.out.println(s1.getName());  // Alice
```

the this keyword refers to the current object — used to distinguish fields from parameters with the same name.

Default constructor: if you define no constructor, Java provides a no-argument constructor. Once you define any constructor, the default is removed.

Multiple constructors (constructor overloading):
```java
    public Student(String name) { this(name, 0); }  // calls other constructor
```

Access levels:
    public    — accessible from any class
    private   — accessible only within the same class
    protected — accessible within the same package and subclasses
    (none)    — package-private: accessible within the same package

Encapsulation pattern — private fields with public getters and setters:
    public class BankAccount {
        private double balance;   // hidden from outside

        public double getBalance() {
            return balance;       // read-only access
        }

        public void deposit(double amount) {
            if (amount > 0) balance += amount;
        }
    }

Why encapsulation matters:
- Protects internal state from invalid direct modification.
- Allows you to change the internal implementation without breaking code that uses the class.
- Enforces validation logic in setters.

final modifier:
- On a variable: makes it a constant (cannot be reassigned).
- On a method: cannot be overridden by subclasses.
- On a class: cannot be subclassed.

Basic inheritance:
    public class Animal {
        String name;
        public void speak() {
            System.out.println(name + " makes a sound.");
        }
    }

    public class Dog extends Animal {
        @Override
        public void speak() {
            System.out.println(name + " barks.");
        }
    }

Key concepts:
- The subclass inherits all non-private members of the superclass.
- @Override annotation: tells the compiler you intend to override a parent method.
- super keyword: accesses the superclass constructor or method.
    super()             — calls parent constructor (must be first line)
    super.methodName()  — calls parent method

Polymorphism:
    Animal a = new Dog();   // Dog object stored in Animal reference
    a.speak();              // calls Dog's speak() — runtime dispatch

Object class: every Java class implicitly extends Object. This is why all objects have toString(), equals(), and hashCode() methods.

Abstract classes: cannot be instantiated — used as base classes:
    public abstract class Shape {
        public abstract double area();   // subclasses must implement
    }

Interface:
    public interface Drawable {
        void draw();           // abstract by default
        default void describe() {
            System.out.println("A drawable object.");
        }
    }

    public class Circle implements Drawable {
        @Override
        public void draw() {
            System.out.println("Drawing circle.");
        }
    }

A class can implement multiple interfaces (unlike extends):
    public class Circle implements Drawable, Resizable { ... }

Abstract class:
    public abstract class Vehicle {
        String brand;
        public abstract void startEngine();   // must override
        public void stop() {                  // concrete method
            System.out.println("Stopping.");
        }
    }

Interface vs abstract class:
| Feature              | Interface        | Abstract Class   |
|----------------------|-----------------|------------------|
| Multiple inheritance | Yes (multiple)   | No (one only)    |
| Constructors         | No               | Yes              |
| Fields               | constants only   | any              |
| Method implementation| default/static  | yes              |

Choose an interface when you want to define a capability shared across unrelated classes. Choose an abstract class when sharing common code among related classes.

