Factorial example:
    public static int factorial(int n) {
        if (n == 0) return 1;          // base case
        return n * factorial(n - 1);   // recursive case
    }

Fibonacci example:
    public static int fib(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) return n;
        return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
    }

How the call stack works:
    factorial(3)
      → 3 * factorial(2)
           → 3 * 2 * factorial(1)
                → 3 * 2 * 1 * factorial(0)
                         → 3 * 2 * 1 * 1 = 6

Each method call is pushed onto the call stack. When the base case is reached, the stack unwinds and results are combined.

StackOverflowError: if the base case is never reached, the stack fills up and Java throws StackOverflowError. Always ensure the recursive call moves toward the base case.

Minimal C program:
    #include <stdio.h>

    int main(void) {
        printf("Hello, World!\n");
        return 0;
    }

Breaking it down:
- #include <stdio.h>: includes the Standard Input/Output header, providing printf and scanf.
- int main(void): the main function returns an integer. void means no command-line arguments.
- { }: curly braces define the function body.
- printf("Hello, World!\n"): prints to the console. \n is a newline character.
- return 0: returns 0 to the operating system, indicating successful execution.

With command-line arguments:
    int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
