Factorial:
    int factorial(int n) {
        if (n == 0) return 1;          // base case
        return n * factorial(n - 1);   // recursive case
    }

Fibonacci:
    int fib(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) return n;
        return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
    }

Trace of factorial(3):
    factorial(3) → 3 * factorial(2)
                    → 3 * 2 * factorial(1)
                          → 3 * 2 * 1 * factorial(0) = 1
                                    → 3 * 2 * 1 = 6

Stack overflow: if the base case is never reached, the call stack fills up. C terminates the program with a segmentation fault or similar signal — there is no StackOverflowError like in Java.

Recursion vs iteration: iteration is usually more memory-efficient in C because every recursive call uses stack space. Prefer iteration for simple loops; use recursion when it naturally matches the problem (tree traversal, backtracking, divide-and-conquer).

