```java
public class Car {
    String brand;
    int    year;
    double price;

    // Default constructor (no parameters)
    Car() {
        brand = "Unknown";
        year  = 2000;
        price = 0.0;
    }

    // Parameterised constructor
    Car(String brand, int year, double price) {
        this.brand = brand;   // 'this' distinguishes field from parameter
        this.year  = year;
        this.price = price;
    }

    // Constructor chaining with this()
    Car(String brand) {
        this(brand, 2024, 20000.0);  // calls the 3-param constructor
    }

    void display() {
        System.out.printf("%s (%d) — $%.0f%n", brand, year, price);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Car c1 = new Car();                        // default
        Car c2 = new Car("Toyota", 2024, 35000);   // parameterised
        Car c3 = new Car("Honda");                 // chained
        c1.display();
        c2.display();
        c3.display();
    }
}
```

Constructors can be **overloaded** — multiple constructors with different parameter lists. `this.field` disambiguates when a parameter has the same name as a field.

File I/O in C follows a four-step pattern:

1. **Open** — `FILE *fp = fopen("file.txt", "w");` opens a file. The second argument is the mode: `"r"` (read), `"w"` (write, creates/truncates), `"a"` (append), `"r+"` (read+write).

2. **Check** — always verify `fp != NULL` before using it.

3. **Read/Write** — use `fprintf(fp, ...)` to write formatted text, `fscanf(fp, ...)` to read formatted text, `fgets(buf, size, fp)` to read a line, `fwrite(buf, size, n, fp)` for binary data.

4. **Close** — `fclose(fp)` flushes the buffer and releases the file handle. Always close files you open.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main(void) {
    FILE *fp = fopen("data.txt", "w");
    if (fp == NULL) { printf("Error opening file\n"); return 1; }
    fprintf(fp, "Hello, file!\n");
    fclose(fp);

    // Read it back
    fp = fopen("data.txt", "r");
    char line[100];
    while (fgets(line, sizeof(line), fp)) printf("%s", line);
    fclose(fp);
    return 0;
}
```
